Silk by Adil Kairolla (MacPaper Engine)
https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine/blob/main/Samples/Silk.macpaper/shaders/silk.metal

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the
top of main.metal for what was changed.

Where the licence is stated
---------------------------
The shader file itself carries no licence header. It was written for the
MacPaper Engine repository, whose LICENSE file (at the repository root,
https://github.com/adilkairolla/macpaper-engine/blob/main/LICENSE) begins:

    "MIT License

    Copyright (c) 2026 Adil Kairolla"

followed by the standard MIT permission notice reproduced below. The
repository's README.md, under "License", says: "MIT © Adil Kairolla". Silk is
one of the repository's own bundled samples (Samples/Silk.macpaper), by the
same author, and every commit in the repository is his.

What else is in this shader, and where it comes from
-----------------------------------------------------
- mp_hash, mp_noise and mp_fbm are copied unchanged from the same repository's
  built-in shader helpers (Sources/MacPaper/Render/ShaderSource.swift), so they
  fall under the same MIT licence. mp_noise is the textbook value noise
  (cubic-smoothed bilinear blend of four lattice hashes). mp_hash is a
  three-line hash whose constants (123.34, 456.21, 45.32) are in wide use in
  other shaders; no credit or licence is attached to it in the MacPaper
  repository and its original author could not be established.
- The q / r / f domain-warp layout, with the offsets (5.2, 1.3), (1.7, 9.2)
  and (8.3, 2.8) and the idea of colouring from f, the size of q and the value
  of r, follows Inigo Quilez's article "Warping",
  https://iquilezles.org/articles/warp/ . The MacPaper file does not credit
  him. His articles index, https://iquilezles.org/articles/ , states: "all
  technical code snippets you'll find are under the MIT license so you can
  easily reuse them, but the mathematical/shader art is protected and requires
  a license for use." Silk reuses the technique and those offsets; its noise,
  warp strengths, time terms, sine-fold shading, sheen and vignette are its own.
  Credit to Inigo Quilez for the technique.

What was changed in this port
-----------------------------
- The MacPaper engine wrapper was removed: effect(Pixel, Frame, Params, Inputs)
  became mainImage, and the three noise helpers were copied in.
- The exposed parameters became constants at their defaults: scale 1.6,
  pulse 1.0, and the default "Orchid" palette #1a0b3d, #6b2fa8, #ff7ab6.
- Speed is 0.5 instead of the default 1.0, so the picture moves more calmly as
  a desktop background.
- The audio level is a constant 0, so the sheen strength is 0.35.
- The engine's top-left, y-down pixel coordinates are rebuilt from our
  bottom-left fragCoord, and the horizontal aspect uses the real output size
  rather than the wallpaper canvas size, so portrait and ultrawide displays are
  not stretched.
The shading maths and every other constant are untouched. Nothing else was
shipped with the original that this folder uses (no images, no textures).

